    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 5207.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 746,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans, and the fluids of all known living organisms. \n\nTo truly understand water, it helps to look at it through a few different lenses: its chemical makeup, its unique physical properties, and its vital role in life and the universe.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Makeup\nChemically, a single molecule of water is made up of **three atoms**: two hydrogen (H) atoms bonded to one oxygen (O) atom. This is why water’s chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. \n\n* **The \"Mickey Mouse\" Shape:** The atoms are arranged in a bent shape (resembling Mickey Mouse ears), which gives the molecule a positive charge on the hydrogen side and a negative charge on the oxygen side. \n* **A Polar Molecule:** Because of this uneven charge, water molecules act like tiny magnets. They attract each other and stick together, which gives water many of its unique behaviors.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature, which flows freely in rivers and oceans.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Unique and Amazing Properties\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid on Earth. These anomalies make it essential for life:\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a delivery system, carrying vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients around the planet and through our bodies.\n* **Ice Floats:** When most liquids freeze, their molecules pack tightly together, making the solid denser than the liquid. Water is the opposite. When water freezes, its molecules lock into an open, crystalline structure that is *less dense* than liquid water. This is why ice floats, preventing lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules love to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which allows water to move up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste.\n* **Ecosystems:** Aquatic ecosystems cover about 71% of the Earth's surface (mostly oceans), providing habitats for millions of species.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through evaporation, condensation (clouds), and precipitation (rain and snow). The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago. \n\nIn summary, water is not just a simple liquid; it is the fundamental driving force of chemistry, weather, geology, and biology on planet Earth."
